        <mxCell id="vQJH2k5jHzZeBWDmTKOx-223" value="Performs queries and mutations" style="ellipse;shape=cloud;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;spacingLeft=20;spacingRight=20;spacingTop=5;" parent="1" vertex="1">
          <mxGeometry x="1260" y="2040" width="187" height="78.5" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="vQJH2k5jHzZeBWDmTKOx-224" value="Same architecture as 2.0, but now it uses GraphQL instead of a Spring Boot REST API. This makes more sense since it is mostly READ related which is the powerful side of GraphQL. The portfolio doesn&#39;t need any input validation and the CMS could have input validation, but that isn&#39;t that necessary since it is just me using it which means the strong point of the REST API isn&#39;t required. GraphQL also still allows input validation if it is really needed.&lt;div&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;b&gt;Advantages compared to 2.0&lt;br&gt;&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;+ Allows requests to be more optimized by limiting the data retrieved from the database.&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;+ All needed data can be retrieved using a single request.&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;b&gt;Disadvantages compared to 2.0&lt;br&gt;&lt;/b&gt;- GraphQL is often more resouce intensive which would increase costs even more.&lt;/div&gt;" style="text;html=1;align=left;verticalAlign=top;whiteSpace=wrap;rounded=0;" parent="1" vertex="1">
          <mxGeometry x="858" y="1220" width="750" height="238.5" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="vQJH2k5jHzZeBWDmTKOx-225" value="GraphQL" style="ellipse;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;" parent="1" vertex="1">
          <mxGeometry x="1458" y="1762.5" width="150" height="80" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
